h2: a client stream closing before its answer ended is reset with CANCEL

A client h2 stream that is closed before the peer ended its answer, eg, a
request its user aborted with lws_set_timeout(LWS_TO_KILL_ASYNC) on a pooled
connection, goes away without telling the server.  The general "swsi got
closed" RST_STREAM in rops_close_kill_connection_h2() is commented out, and
the resets upstream added there (35c77bb96b "h2: a stream closing before its
answer ended is reset with CANCEL", and the "answered before the request
ended" one) are only for server streams.  So the server keeps producing and
sending the answer for a stream nobody reads (the client drops it as
straggling DATA), for ever if it never ends, and the stream stays open on the
connection until the connection itself closes.  On txiki.js, about 23 such
aborts on one pooled connection while another stream stays open make the next
request on it fail.

Reset such a stream with RST_STREAM(CANCEL), as 35c77bb96b does for a server
stream abandoning its answer: a client mux substream whose peer has not ended
its side (h2.END_STREAM, latched as the END_STREAM is consumed), that is not
IDLE (never sent, nothing to reset) or CLOSED (already reset either way, or
done with), on a connection that is not itself closing, which says it for
every stream.  A request whose answer ended normally is not reset.  If the
reset can't be queued, it is logged, as for the server streams.

lws_h2_rst_stream() latches on the stream's own h2.rst_queued on v5.1.0, not
on the parser's frame type, so a close outside the parser (at POLLOUT, from
a timeout) queues it.

Not an upstream backport: v5.1.0 (5f39aa483) and main (095ee0784d) still
never reset a client stream.  It is the client-side counterpart of upstream
35c77bb96b.  Upstreamable.

---
diff --git a/lib/sansio/h2/ops-h2.c b/lib/sansio/h2/ops-h2.c
index 1d4e00af1..279e6ed41 100644
--- a/lib/sansio/h2/ops-h2.c
+++ b/lib/sansio/h2/ops-h2.c
@@ -921,6 +921,29 @@ rops_close_kill_connection_h2(struct lws *wsi, enum lws_close_status reason)
 			lwsl_wsi_info(wsi, "%s: couldn't queue RST_STREAM",
 				      __func__);
 	}
+#if defined(LWS_WITH_CLIENT)
+	else if (wsi->client_mux_substream &&
+		 !wsi->h2.END_STREAM &&
+		 wsi->h2.h2_state != LWS_H2_STATE_CLOSED &&
+		 wsi->h2.h2_state != LWS_H2_STATE_IDLE &&
+		 wsi->mux.parent_wsi &&
+		 !lwsi_close_started(wsi->mux.parent_wsi)) {
+		/*
+		 * The client counterpart: the stream is going away before the
+		 * peer ended its answer, eg, the user aborted the request.
+		 * Without a reset the server would go on producing the answer
+		 * for a stream nobody reads, for ever if it never ends, and
+		 * keep the stream open on the connection.  A stream never
+		 * sent (IDLE) has nothing to reset, nor has one the peer
+		 * ended or reset, nor one whose connection is closing, which
+		 * says it for every stream.
+		 */
+		if (lws_h2_rst_stream(wsi, H2_ERR_CANCEL,
+				      "request abandoned"))
+			lwsl_wsi_info(wsi, "%s: couldn't queue RST_STREAM",
+				      __func__);
+	}
+#endif
 
 	lwsl_info(" %s, his parent %s: siblings:\n", lws_wsi_tag(wsi), lws_wsi_tag(wsi->mux.parent_wsi));
 	lws_wsi_mux_dump_children(wsi);
